## Q4.5 Testing
For each of the following, discuss and perform analysis:

### (a) Load the dataset Heart.Monitor.csv
A study of a new electronic heart monitor was performed on 68 men aged from 50 to 80 years
old. Each participant was fitted with the new monitor and the currently used monitor. Due to the
unusual amount of equipment and wires, participants were given a few hours to become accustom
to wearing the monitors before measurements were taken. Is there any difference between the
measured heart rates of the two monitors?

```{r Heart Monitor}

heart <- read.csv("Heart.Monitor.csv")
head(heart)
boxplot(heart, data=heart)
t.test(x = heart$Old, y = heart$New, 
       alternative = "t",
       mu = 0, paired = TRUE, var.equals = FALSE,
       conf.level = 0.95)
# in paired t test we use x - y, so old - new: there is evidence against
# the H0 (at 5% level) that the two monitors measure the same heart rate 
# OR: shows a significant (at 5% level) difference between two monitors
#with mean = -1.41 [95% CI -1.96 to -0.85]

Data <- read.csv("Heart.Monitor.csv")

difference <- Data$Old - Data$New
average <- (Data$Old + Data$New) / 2

bias <- mean(difference)
sd_diff <- sd(difference)

lower <- bias - 1.96 * sd_diff
upper <- bias + 1.96 * sd_diff
plot(average, difference,
     xlab = "Average heart rate (bpm)",
     ylab = "Old - New (bpm)",
     main = "Bland-Altman Plot")

abline(h = bias, col = "blue", lwd = 2)
abline(h = c(lower, upper),
       col = "red", lty = 2)
abline(h = 0, col = "gray")

```


### (b) Load the dataset White.Cell.csv
A study of white blood cells counts in HIV positive patients with or without co-infection
with hepititus C was conducted. Thirty co-infected and thirty mono-infected patients were en-
rolled in the study. Do co-infected patients have a different white blood cell count compared to
mono-infected?
```{r White Cell, echo=TRUE}
cells <- read.csv("White.Cell.csv")

head(cells)
dim(cells)
boxplot(cells)
t.test(cells$Mono, cells$Co,
       alternative = "two.sided",
       mu = 0,
       paired = FALSE, var.equal = FALSE)
```
With thiry subjects in each group, there is no evidence against the H0 (at 5% level) that there is no difference between the two groups.

### (c) Load the dataset Twins.csv
A study of teenage depression was conducted across the UK. Eighty twins aged 15–19 were
recruited, using an interview and questionnaire the study determined their depression score on a
0–100 scale. Is there any evidence of a difference in female and male depression scores?
(Hint: this question involves some additional data manipulation)

```{r Twins non paired, echo = TRUE}

twins <-  read.csv("Twins.csv")
head(twins)
twins.male <- subset(twins, Gender == "Male", select = c(Id, Gender, Score))
twins.female <- subset(twins, Gender == "Female", select = c(Id, Gender, Score))
twins.male
twins.female
t.test(twins.male$Score, twins.female$Score,
       alternative = "two.sided",
       mu = 0, var.equal = FALSE)
```


```{r Twins paired, echo=TRUE}
NEW <- subset(twins, Same =="No")
head(NEW)

NEW.F <- subset(NEW, Gender == "Female", select = c("Id", "Score"))
NEW.M <- subset(NEW, Gender == "Male", select = c("Id", "Score"))

WIDE.F <- rename(NEW.F, Score.Female = Score)
WIDE.M <- rename(NEW.M, Score.Male = Score)

WIDE <- merge(WIDE.F, WIDE.M, by = c("Id"))
head(WIDE)
  
t.test(WIDE$Score.Female, WIDE$Score.Male, 
       paired = TRUE)
```
